---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import butlerImage1 from "../imgs/gender-performativity/pt1.jpg";
import butlerImage2 from "../imgs/gender-performativity/pt2.png";

import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const pageTitle = "aurabora.org";
const pageDescription = "an explanation of my website.";
---

<BaseLayout pageTitle={pageTitle} pageDescription={pageDescription}>
  <p>
    aurabora.org is a simple website that serves as the hub for my blogposts and
    a collection of resources i find to be particularly useful. Its use may grow
    overtime so be on the lookout for changes.
  </p>

  <h2>blogposts</h2>
  <p>
    I leave here informal pieces of writing that mostly exists for me to share
    my unfinished thoughts as I learn more.
  </p>
  <p>Generally, there are two types of blogposts I tend to write:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>More thought-out pieces usually referencing my reading</li>
    <li>Rants/vents of my feelings and/or loose thoughts</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Please keep in mind that these, at the end of the day, are informal pieces
    of writing, and I am not interested in treating them like academic writing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, I would like to say that my blogposts are a record of my growth and
    learning, and as such, I may not fully agree with everything I have written
    prior. This is, frankly speaking, a personal frustration of mine, but I
    cannot rewrite every blogpost I've ever written. I ask that you read each
    blogpost with a grain of salt, and, if you know me personally, perhaps talk
    with me about any doubts and critiques you may have.
  </p>

  <h2>library</h2>
  <p>
    I sometimes add any resources I found interesting that I used in my
    blogposts. Maybe at some point I'll expand this, but for now it's pretty
    limited.
  </p>

  <h2>who am I?</h2>
  <p>
    I'm a college student studying anthropology, linguistics, queerness, and race,
    with a particular interest in trans medicine & trans sovereignty, as well
    as love, especially as it relates to power & liberation. I
    consider myself to be a highly well rounded person, however, with interests
    in fields across society that relate to my person and those of my friends,
    as well as a whole host of creative forms.
  </p>
  <p>
    As a queer trans person <em>(she/it pronouns)</em>, I'm naturally
    interested in many of these fields that have shaped my own personal identity
    and continue to shape my existence within society.
  </p>
  <p>
    Above all else, the goal of my writing, and the goal of theory more broadly,
    is liberation. Here, that means both hermeneutical liberation (through
    undertanding oppression, injustice, privilege, ethics, and ontology(s)),
    but also building a model of practice that informs how we may structure 
    life moving forward to create care and love in our lives.
  </p>
  <p>
    I want us to be able to create liberation in the here and now, and my work is an
    attempt at furthering that. For an in-depth look at this subject, especially
    in regards to building our lives in these means towards these ends, I
    implore you to read my post on <a href="/blogposts/post-17"
      >Socipolitical Creativity.</a
    >
    <p>
      My hopes for this website are to open your mind to other possibilities in
      life, to broaden your understanding of who you are and the roads that have
      brought <strong>us</strong> here together, and to begin to help chart a path
      elsewhere.
    </p>

    <h2>extras</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://www.pictureofhotdog.com"
        >a picture of a hotdog for your enjoyment</a
      >
    </p>
    <p>
      Judith Butler's theory of gender performativity, explained with cats (<a
        href="https://www.themarysue.com/judith-butler-explained-with-cats/"
        >Source</a
      >)
      <Image
        src={butlerImage1}
        alt='cat: Reading Judith Butler hurts my brain. What on earth does "performativity" actually mean!?
              Butler: I can help! Let me start from the beginning...
              Butler: See, there is no "interior truth" to gender identity, because sex is "always already gender"
              cat: You lost me at "the beginning"―why is there no inner essence to gender identity?
              Butler: Because gender is a result of repeated "styles of the flesH" that "congeal over time". This process makes us think that there is a natural inner truth―"the construction compels our belief in its necessity and naturalness"
              cat: But if there is no inner gender essence, riddle me this―how is sex *already* gender? Dont we have lady bits and man bits and that is a scientific FACT?
              Butler: It comes down to the "heterosexual matrix"
              The cat imagines Neo from the Matrix dodging bullets
              Butler: Yeeeah, not quite...'
                class="chart"
      />
      <Image
        src={butlerImage2}
        alt="the second part of butler's theory of gender performance explained with cats"
        class="chart"
      />
    </p>
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
